
 Name: dsquasar - back up RDA/GDEX data archives onto the Quasar backup servers

 Check the Backup flags set in dataset.backflag and/or dsgroup.backflag of RDADB
 to gather Web and Saved files, tar them into larger (>= 2GB) backup files, and
 back them up onto the NCAR GDEX Quasar Globus end points, for a Backup copy
 (gdex-quasar) and/or a disaster-recovery Drdata copy (gdex-quasar-drdata).  The
 file tarring and transferring is performed through the utility program dsarch.

 Usage: dsquasar [-b] [(-a | -t DatasetIDs)] [-c ChangeDays] [-(e|E)]     \
                 [-(B|D)] [-A ActionBits] [-l (Y|N)] [-m ProcessCount]    \
                 [-n] [-u] [-d [HostName] [TryCount]] [-h]

   Either -a or -t must be given (usage is displayed otherwise).  dsquasar runs
   from the Quasar backup work directory; it changes there automatically.


 DATASET SELECTION

   -a     Gather files of all datasets that have a Backup flag set.

   -t  DatasetIDs
          One or more dataset IDs whose files are backed up.  The SQL wildcard
          '%' is allowed and is expanded against the dataset table.  Ignored,
          with a warning, when -a is also given.


 BACKUP SCOPE

   -B     Quasar Backup only (endpoint gdex-quasar) for the given datasets.

   -D     Quasar Backup & Drdata (gdex-quasar and gdex-quasar-drdata).

          -B and -D are mutually exclusive; the default (neither given) backs up
          to both the Backup and the Drdata endpoints.

   -c  ChangeDays
          Used with -A 1 to re-back up files that changed within the given
          number of days.  Must be greater than 0.


 ACTIONS

   -A  ActionBits
          Bit flags selecting the actions to perform; the valid values are
          1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 16 and it defaults to 3 (actions 1 + 2):

             1  - create the dsarch input Save/Web file lists and add the
                  backup file (bfile) placeholder records (status 'N').
             2  - build tar files (each >= 5GB where possible) from the Saved/
                  Web files listed in the input files (status 'N' -> 'T').
             4  - transfer the built tar files (batched to >= 90GB per Globus
                  transfer) to the Quasar backup end points (status 'T' -> 'A').
             8  - check file sizes between RDADB and the Quasar servers, and
                  reset status to 'N' when they differ.
            16  - dump backup statistics for files backed up and files that are
                  ready to be backed up.

          Combined values: 3 = 1+2, 6 = 2+4, 7 = 1+2+4.


 RUN CONTROL

   -b     Turn on background mode; no screen display.

   -d  [HostName] [TryCount]
          Turn on delay mode to run as a batch job.  A dscheck record is created
          and the dscheck daemon later submits the job to PBS.  On the initial
          submit dsquasar sizes multi-processing from the detail file count and
          reserves matching PBS cpus (walltime + select=1:ncpus=N:mem=Ngb) in the
          dscheck qoptions; the invocation arguments are left unchanged so a
          repeat submit with the same arguments still blocks as a duplicate.

   -l  (Y|N)
          Whether to check and lock a dataset before backing it up.  Defaults
          to Y.

   -m  ProcessCount
          Number of concurrent child processes.  Defaults to 1.  In delay mode
          the process count travels through the reserved PBS cpus rather than
          the command line.

   -n     Gather and show the available file counts only; no backup actions are
          performed.

   -W  MaxWorkers
          Maximum number of concurrent batch workers for the same command.
          Defaults to 2.  A repeat delayed submit normally blocks while the
          batch job is running; if that job has run for more than 6 hours and
          has still completed no more than 1% of its recorded work, it is
          treated as stalled and an extra worker is submitted instead, up to
          this limit.  Extra workers rely on dataset locking to stay off each
          other's files, so this is pinned to 1 with -l N, and also for the
          transfer action (-A 4, 6, 7), which claims tar files without locking.

   -w  WorkerSlot
          Worker slot number for this run, added automatically to an extra
          worker's command line so it gets its own dscheck record.  Not
          normally set by hand.  Even slots walk the datasets in descending
          dsid order (from d999999) and odd slots in ascending order, so that a
          pair of workers meets in the middle instead of contending for the
          same dataset locks.

   -u     Clean up (unlock) backup locks for the given dataset IDs.  Dataset IDs
          must be provided (not valid with -a).


 EMAIL

   -e     Send an email to the specialist with backup statistics.

   -E     Send an email to the specialist with detailed backup statistics.


 MISCELLANEOUS

   -h     Display this help document.


 EXAMPLES

   1. Back up (create input files and tar files for) all flagged datasets:

         dsquasar -a -A 3

   2. Transfer built tar files of one dataset to both Quasar end points:

         dsquasar -t d123456 -A 4

   3. Show the counts of files ready to back up for a dataset, without acting:

         dsquasar -t d123456 -n

   4. Dump backup statistics for all datasets by email:

         dsquasar -a -A 16 -e

   5. Queue a delayed PBS batch job to create input files and tar files for all
      datasets in the background (as run from cron); multi-processing is sized
      automatically from the file count:

         dsquasar -a -A 3 -e -b -d PBS

   6. Unlock backup locks left on datasets:

         dsquasar -t d123456 -u
